// test-execution
// test-adr: 029
// Issue #1491: a variable typed by an INCLUDED function-as-type was rejected.
//
// ADR-029 makes every function declaration a type and draws no distinction
// between a function declared here and one reached through an include. But
// FunctionCallAnalyzer.isCallbackType answered "is this a callback type?" by
// asking `allLocalFunctions`, a set that exists to EXCLUDE cross-file functions
// because ADR-030 define-before-use ordering (#786) needs that distinction.
// One set, two questions -- so calling through the variable was rejected with
// `E0422: function 'viaInclude' called before definition`, for valid C-Next.
//
// The same-file pair is the CONTROL, not decoration: it is the byte-identical
// shape with the type's function declared locally. It was accepted before the
// fix and must keep passing, so a failure in the cross-file pair is
// attributable to the include alone rather than to function-as-type support
// in general.
//
// Both the DECLARATION and the CALL are exercised. The declaration alone was
// already accepted before the fix, so a fixture that only declared the variable
// would pass while the bug was live -- presence is not proof (#1222).
//
// `sharedNotify` covers the void case deliberately: the visible-here set the
// fix reads is `functionReturnTypes`, and a reader could reasonably assume a
// map named for return types skips functions that return none. It does not.

#include "func-lib.cnx"

u32 localHelper() {
    return 5;
}

u32 main() {
    // Control: same-file function-as-type, declared and called.
    // MISRA C:2012 Rule 13.5 forbids a call in an `if` condition, so every
    // result is bound first and then compared.
    localHelper ownRef <- localHelper;
    u32 ownResult <- ownRef();
    if (ownResult != 5) return 1;

    // The assertion: cross-file function-as-type. The declaration was accepted
    // before the fix; the call was not.
    sharedHelper viaInclude <- sharedHelper;
    u32 includedResult <- viaInclude();
    if (includedResult != 7) return 2;

    // Both reached through their variables, pinning that the two paths produce
    // the same shape rather than merely both compiling.
    u32 combined <- ownResult + includedResult;
    if (combined != 12) return 3;

    // A void function is a type too, and is callable through a variable.
    sharedNotify viaVoid <- sharedNotify;
    viaVoid();

    return 0;
}
